- d21 `internal/cli/scripts/guarded-group.sh` cleanup_group (TERM grace loop and post-KILL group-reap loop) plus new now_ms/grace_deadline helpers: **rewrite**. Upstream 9c9b79a1 (R21.6) made the TERM grace stop early when the workload exits, but bounded it by a poll count: ticks=ceil(grace_ms/100) with each tick running `ps` plus `sleep .1`. On a loaded host each tick costs far more than 100ms, so the grace stretched (measured 5.7-6.5s for grace_ms=1000 against the test's <=3s bound). Both loops are now bounded by elapsed time: a millisecond deadline is taken before the loop and re-read each iteration, so a slow poll can only end the loop sooner, never extend it. The clock is EPOCHREALTIME (Bash 5, the WSL2/Linux helper interpreter) with a /proc/uptime fallback (monotonic, WSL-safe, 10ms) and a whole-second `date +%s` last resort; the source's own resolution is added to the deadline so a coarse clock can never cut the grace short. No `date +%s%N` assumption (BSD/macOS date lacks %N). R21.6 semantics are preserved: the liveness check still runs before any sleep, so already-completed work waits zero, and still-live work waits the full configured grace. The post-KILL reap loop keeps its original nominal bound (grace_ms) but is now wall-clock bounded too.
- d22 `internal/cli/ssh_wsl_grace_linux_test.go` TestGuardedGroupGraceBoundedByElapsedTime (new test); TestWSL2CleanupTermGrace unchanged: **rewrite**. Adds a focused regression test for the root cause without touching the existing end-to-end bounds: TestWSL2CleanupTermGrace keeps its 1s/2s/3s limits and is not skipped. The new test drives the real embedded guarded-group.sh functions with a liveness probe that costs 200ms and a 1s grace, and asserts the loop polls about grace/probe times (<=7) rather than once per 100ms of grace (10). It fails on the pre-fix script (10 polls, 3.13s) and passes on the fix (4 polls, 1.26s), and the poll count can only fall on a loaded host, so it is not load-flaky. It mirrors upstream's TestTermGraceOnlyWaitsForLiveWork, which pins the same elapsed-time-not-poll-count property for the Go supervisor.
- d23 `internal/providers/agentsandbox/ssh_backend.go` sshLeaseBackend.Resolve — repository-ownership authorization (authorizeAgentSandboxRepoClaim call): **combined**. The merge kept the fork's sshLeaseBackend.Resolve (L6/L13 fast paths, identity fencing) but took upstream's 643684be18 signature of authorizeAgentSandboxRepoClaim(claim, repoRoot, reclaim, legacyRoot ...string), which now admits a stored claim root that literally equals the invoking command's own pre-canonicalization root via core.ClaimRepositoryMatches. The fork's call site was left at the old two-argument form, so Resolve refused a lease claimed at the invocation's legacy logical root when the command resolved through the canonical physical root. Passing req.Repo.LegacyRoot restores R14's semantics exactly: canonical root matches, or the single literal legacy root the invoking command itself looked up; ClaimRepositoryMatches compares literal strings only and never re-resolves the stored path, so a stored path reached only through a different alias is still refused with the usual claim-owner error, and --reclaim/identity fencing are untouched. Every other call site in the package (backend.go:229, claim.go:159) already passes the legacy root, so this makes Resolve consistent with them.
- d24 `internal/cli/run_download.go` downloadRemoteFileWithLimits + new remoteDownloadStreamError helper: **rewrite**. Upstream 471eaf58 changed runSSHStreamResult to map a signaled ssh child to 128+signal (SIGTERM 143, SIGKILL 137) instead of -1 (R21.6). The fork's download path still reported the raw stream error it saw through the io.Pipe ("read size header: signal: terminated") and dropped the mapped status, so a signaled download never named the status the run path reports. The download now derives its remote failure from the transport's (code, err) result: a child the transport saw exit is reported as "remote command exited <code>" (143 for SIGTERM), a transport failure keeps its own error, and a staging failure independent of the stream failure is still appended. Bounded download behaviour is untouched: the same io.Pipe staging, size header, max-bytes and disk-reserve limits, temp-file removal on failure and publish-on-success, and the remote stderr diagnostic buffer is still appended to the error.
